    "india": {
      "type": "story-chapter",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "The chai was never the point",
        "body": "<p>After graduating I spent several months in India, and the way people drink chai there was impossible to miss. Not sit-down cafés — roadside stalls, gallons of it on the boil all day, for anyone walking past.</p><p>The chaiwalas were holding something together. They knew their regulars by name. None of it was transactional.</p><p>American coffee culture doesn't ask anything of the person serving you, or of the person being served. I think that's part of why this country is as lonely as it is.</p>",
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/DSC03473.jpg",
        "image_alt": "Steam rising off a tall pot of chai on the boil",
        "caption": "Gallons on the boil, Portland version.",
        "media_side": "start",
        "color_scheme": "scheme-9",
        "padding-block-start": 96,
        "padding-block-end": 96
      }
    },
